<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paul Cézanne</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1839">1839</a>–<a href="/years/1906">1906</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Cézanne, Pau Cesana</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Paul Cézanne was a French Post-Impressionist painter from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aix-en-provence">Aix-en-Provence</a> whose work formed the bridge between late 19th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/impressionism">Impressionism</a> and early 20th-century Cubism. The son of a banker, he was a school friend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emile-zola">Émile Zola</a>. Through close study of Impressionist forms he arrived at a new pictorial language, building up complex fields from planes of colour and small brushstrokes and emphasising the underlying structure of objects. His paintings at first met ridicule, until Ambroise Vollard gave him a first solo exhibition in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1895. He died of pneumonia in Aix on 22 October 1906.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paul-cezanne.jpg" alt="Paul Cézanne" width="250" height="333" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown; Cézanne was born in 1839 and died in 1906 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paul-Cezanne.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At the Café Guerbois in Paris their discussions were often led by Édouard Manet, and they were joined by Camille Pissarro, <b>Paul Cézanne</b> and Armand Guillaumin.</blockquote><a href="/movement/impressionism">Impressionism · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
